  that whenever I did make it to the boss  I was hardly progressing never landing  more than a single-digit number of hits  before being flattened like a pancake or  flung off the edge of the wall I  eventually got so fed up that I had to  put the game down I would return  maybe in a day a couple of days a week  and try again the process would repeat  and I would take another break from the  game run fight die run fight die run  fight die repeat repeat repeat months  went by and I still hadn't gotten past  the game's first real ball I was upset  at the boss assuredly I didn't think the  bridge was a big enough marina and there  were some massive clipping problems due  to horses size relative to the bridge  they being similar widths but what I was  more frustrated at was how the vast  majority of my time in this endeavor was  not spent learning how to defeat this  boss and growing more accustomed to his  moveset it was spent running from  bonfire to fog wall because each  instance of time of the running was much  greater than the amount of time I lasted  inside the boss fight the running was  not enjoyable gameplay  nor was it engaging that was memorizing  a short button sequence and having to  repeat it every time I wanted to try and  beat tourists in the end I was probably  very close to dropping the game for good  but luckily through a friend or some  guide online I learned that Taurus is  super easy to kill if you go up the  ladder and plunging attack in ad mortem  after learning this tourist took me one  or two more tries  and it was a rushed have finally  vanquished the boss that delayed me for  months and actually get to explore the  rest of the game  meet Solaire and discover branching  world paths exhilarating now if I am  honest the added requirement of having  to make the run back to Taurus which  isn't even a particularly long run in  regard to the series did make the  accomplishment of beating him even  sweeter it made each attempt a bit  harder because I had to do something  else to get the chance to fight torts  but it did not achieve this in a good  way or I should say a way that I liked  it did not achieve this through giving  me engage in combat it did not achieve  this through giving me a thoughtful  challenge it did not achieve this  through giving me a learning opportunity  it achieved this by making me hold  forward on the analog stick and pressing  the B button sure I turn the camera a  few times but that was all the run up to  the boss amounted to it's all the run up  to any boss amounts to an amazingly  basic button combination that must be  executed for around a minute every time  I wish to challenge your boss now I  could fight every enemy every single  times I make that would make the  gameplay more engaging and complex to  some degree but the amount of time that  it would take to fight every preceding  enemy every time I ever challenged a  boss in a souls game would probably be  analogous to the entirety of the time I  put into the series as a whole as  example I must have died to the abyss  Watchers and Dark Souls 3 upwards of 30  times so what good would it have done to  stop and kill all the grooves in that  situation every time I would not have  benefited in any significant way from  farming basic enemies over and over and  over after the entire preceding swamp  and the first two to three runs I would  know their move set and can expect what  they'll do you could say I would level  up a few times making the boss easier  but my problem with most bosses is not  that I don't do enough damage or an  underleveled sometimes that's the case  but more often than not I just don't  know their kit well enough and the only  way to learn their kit and moveset is to  fight the boss so the only sensible  option I am left with is running past  these meaningless enemies time and time  again  many if not most of these runs are not  hard the run to the abyss Watchers is a  great example of that it's practically a  straight line with one left turn at the  end and you're at the boss it's trivial  to complete so if it's so easy why not  just do it well part of the reason is  because it is so easy run ups to fog  walls equate to a bad game of Dance  Dance Revolution or osoo where instead  of a consistent string of commands that  I have to input in precise order lining  up with music or what have you the whole  game is just up on the left stick and  hold B maybe sometimes press B maybe  sometimes slightly swerve the right  stick for convenience of uni and that is  about the entirety of engagement I feel  in these run ups  Dark Souls 2 has a prime example of the  futility of this mechanic because of  course Dark Souls 2 does that I think  will garner some sympathy for my  position the run-up to the final boss in  Dark Souls 2 includes a door that you  have to wait a bit to open and a long  narrow walkway with no enemies to open  the door you have to equip a ring which  serves no other function so for the  final fight you'll want to switch that  ring slot back to something useful this  is an inventory process you'll do every  time you make an attempt on the boss and  then you run for about a minute going in  essentially a straight line  and then you get to the boss fight if  you're like me you'll die and get to  repeat this whole process if you die  again repeat again every time learning  nothing new in the minutes wasted by the  game forcing you to open the door and  run back to the fog wall if someone can  defend this please do it makes the bill  up to the first fight the mattock yes  but any secondary attempts is rendered  annoying horribly so by this long  pointless walk funnily enough at this  moment I still haven't beaten dark souls  twos final boss because of this  pointless pathway I got so fed up with  the walk that I haven't bothered to make  it again I don't need extra time to  think about the bosses attacks or a fury  to cool off  I can do both of those things without  any help from the game so these run bags  don't need to be there for that reason  it also comes down to one of the core  dichotomies in the soul series and that  is between world exploration and boss  fights you can split any of the games up  into these two categories and see two of  the main focuses from soft crafted  experiences based around long tent  sections of world and level exploration  followed up by climactic boss fights the  idea that one has to repeat a small  chunk of the world exploration side of  things  every time they fail and want to retry a  boss fight attempt it doesn't make much  sense to me especially when it's an  intensely neutered version of that  exploration because it is simply running  through the level and not actually  exploring it and the games are often  designed around the player dying two  bosses at least a couple of times so to  forcibly tacked on a wanting version of  the world exploration to every boss  fight doesn't seem to fit I can't  entertain any real reason to have these  run back I mean if someone can defend  the design choice of the minute plus  straight walk at the end of Dark Souls 2  please please do write a comment make a  video anything I felt this way the first  time I play the original Dark Souls and  I felt this way with each successive  title I've taken a crack at it  unfortunately has never gotten better  now a bit contrary to what some let you  think I'm not going to be offering a  solution it would be a bit arrogant of  me to try and claim one without taking  into account every single other game  design choice and consequence such a  shift in an integral part of the series  would cause and that would be much more  content than even a whole nother video  could contain so I do not end this video  with a solution resolution conclusion or  anything else like that just a thank you  for taking the time to listen to my case  against the worst feature in my eyes in  this beloved franchise I care about the  series so I like discussion about it  hopefully this can be a starting point  for some new discussions about one of  the most contentious issues I have with  the game especially conversations that  don't end with an unnecessarily  condescending and get good  for those of you that like my lower  explanations of soul stuff be on the  lookout I'm planning a video talking  about general lore and context for each  and every boss for Dark Souls remastered  it won't be super in-depth but more of a  look at why each boss is standing in  your way in a bit of their backstory  thank you for watching
 

